      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one. A function is one-to-one if every horizontal line intersects its graph at most once.\n\nLet's analyze each graph:\n\n1. **Graph A**:\n   - Horizontal lines of the form \\( y = C \\) (where \\( C \\) is a constant) intersect the graph at most once.\n   - This suggests that the function \\( f(x) \\) is one-to-one.\n   - Therefore, \\( f(x) = x^3/40 + x^2/20 - x/2 + 2 \\) has an inverse.\n\n2. **Graph B**:\n   - The function is \\( y = -x \\) (a horizontal line).\n   - It intersects the graph at many points (e.g., at \\( (10, -10) \\) and \\( (-10, 10) \\)).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(x) = -x \\) is not one-to-one.\n\n3. **Graph C**:\n   - The function is \\( y = \\arccos(x - 2) \\).\n   - As \\( x \\) varies, \\( y \\) varies continuously.\n   - This function is not one-to-one because it can take a value \\( y \\) with multiple \\( x \\)-values (e.g., \\( y = 0.564 \\) has two \\( x \\)-values in the range \\([-1, 3]\\)).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(x) = \\arccos(x - 2) \\) is not one-to-one.\n\n4. **Graph D**:\n   - The function is \\( y = x^2 + 4 \\) reflected over the line \\( y = x \\).\n   - Since it's a parabola that opens upwards, it is strictly increasing on the interval \\([-2, \\infty)\\).\n   - Therefore, \\( f(x) = x^2 + 4 \\) is one-to-one.\n   - Hence, \\( f(x) = x^2 + 4 \\) has an inverse.\n\n5. **Graph E**:\n   - The function is \\( y = x^4 \\).\n   - Since any vertical line will intersect it at the same point (8), this function is not one-to-one.\n   - Therefore, \\( f(x) = x^4 \\) does not have an inverse.\n\nSo, the functions that have inverses are Graph A and Graph D. The final answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{A, D}\\)\n\nLet's confirm this analysis with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variables and functions\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the functions\r\nf1 = x**3 / 40 + x**2 / 20 - x / 2 + 2\r\nf2 = -x\r\nf3 = sp.acos(x - 2)\r\nf4 = x**2 + 4\r\n\r\n# Check if the functions are one-to-one by examining their derivatives\r\ndef is_one_to_one(func):\r\n    derivative = sp.diff(func, x)\r\n    return sp.simplify(sp.solve(derivative, x))\r\n\r\n# Get the derivatives\r\nderivatives = [is_one_to_one(f1), is_one_to_one(f2), is_one_to_one(f3), is_one_to_one(f4)]\r\n\r\nprint(derivatives)\n```",
